\title[Boolean Gram dynamics]{Boolean Gram Closure as a Finite Dynamical System}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\B=\{0,1\}$ with Boolean addition and multiplication, and iterate
$\Gamma_n(A)=AA^{\mathsf T}$ on the $n\times n$ Boolean matrices.  We give
the complete finite functional graph.  If $G=AA^{\mathsf T}$, then
$\Gamma_n^t(A)=G^{2^{t-1}}$ for $t\geq1$.  Thus every orbit ends at the
looped clique completion of the active components of the row-intersection
graph.  The recurrent states are precisely partial equivalence relations,
all fixed; a nonfixed source has depth
$1+\lceil\log_2 D(G)\rceil$, with a zero logarithmic term for $D(G)\leq1$.
The sharp carrier height is zero at $n=1$ and
$1+\lceil\log_2(n-1)\rceil$ for $n\geq2$.  There are $B_{n+1}$ fixed
states.  Independently, for every prescribed target we count the complete
ordered-column fibre by inclusion--exclusion over its loop and edge
requirements.  The image criterion is a loop-sensitive edge-clique-cover
bound.  Boolean powers, transitive closure, graph intersection
representations, clique covers, and symmetric Boolean factorization are
treated as prior mechanisms; the note remains on external hold.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{The literal map and its graph factor}

Write $\B^{n\times n}$ for the set of zero--one matrices, with
\[
 (PQ)_{ij}=\bigvee_{r=1}^{n}(P_{ir}\wedge Q_{rj}).
\]
Our self-map is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:literal}
 \Gm:\B^{n\times n}\longrightarrow\B^{n\times n},
 \qquad \Gm(A)=AA^{\mathsf T}.
\end{equation}
If $R_i=\{r:A_{ir}=1\}$ is row $i$ regarded as a subset of $[n]$, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intersection}
 \Gm(A)_{ij}=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad R_i\cap R_j\ne\varnothing.
\end{equation}
Hence $G=\Gm(A)$ is a symmetric relation.  A vertex $i$ has a loop exactly
when $R_i$ is nonempty, and every off-diagonal edge has loops at both ends.
We call these vertices \emph{active}.  On the active vertices, $G$ is an
ordinary undirected graph with a loop at each vertex; inactive vertices are
unlooped and isolated.

The interpretation \eqref{eq:intersection} is the classical set-intersection
representation of a graph.  Its equivalence with an edge clique cover goes
back at least to Szpilrajn--Marczewski and Erd\H{o}s--Goodman--P\'osa
\cite{SzpilrajnMarczewski1945,ErdosGoodmanPosa1966}.  Boolean matrix powers
and transitive-closure algorithms are also classical
\cite{Warshall1962,Kim1982}.  More specifically, Fitting records the
monotone chain
$(AA^{\mathsf T})\leq(AA^{\mathsf T})^2\leq\cdots$ and examples with
arbitrarily many strict inclusions as dimension grows
\cite[Theorem~8 and Example~9]{Fitting2003}.  Exact symmetric Boolean
factorization $M=WW^{\mathsf T}$ and its clique-cover and hypergraph
interpretations are explicit in recent algorithmic work
\cite{ChenSongTaoZhang2022}.  These mechanisms are background here.  Our
scope is the exact functional graph of the literal self-map
\eqref{eq:literal}, paired with all of its finite ordered-column fibres; no
priority claim is made.

For a finite self-map, $\depth(A)$ is the least $d\geq0$ for which
$\Gm^d(A)$ is recurrent.  Put $D(G)=0$ when $G$ has no active edge between
distinct vertices; otherwise $D(G)$ is the largest diameter of an active
connected component.

\begin{theorem}[complete temporal classification]\label{thm:temporal}
Let $A\in\B^{n\times n}$ and $G=AA^{\mathsf T}$.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item For every $t\geq1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:iterate}
 \Gm^t(A)=G^{2^{t-1}}.
\end{equation}
The endpoint is obtained by replacing every active connected component of
$G$ by a fully looped clique and leaving every inactive vertex unlooped and
isolated.

\item The recurrent states are exactly the partial equivalence relations:
disjoint unions of fully looped cliques together with unlooped isolated
vertices.  Every recurrent state is fixed.  Their number is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bell}
 |\Fix(\Gm)|=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom nk B_k=B_{n+1},
\end{equation}
where $B_k$ is the $k$th Bell number.  Consequently the Artin--Mazur zeta
function is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta}
 \zeta_{\Gm}(z)=(1-z)^{-B_{n+1}}.
\end{equation}

\item The exact source-dependent clock is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:clock}
 \depth(A)=
 \begin{cases}
 0,&A\text{ is a partial equivalence relation},\\[1mm]
 1+\lceil\log_2 D(G)\rceil,&\text{otherwise},
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where the ceiling term is declared to be zero for $D(G)\leq1$.
The sharp height of the whole carrier is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:height}
 h_n=\max_A\depth(A)=
 \begin{cases}
 0,&n=1,\\
 1+\lceil\log_2(n-1)\rceil,&n\geq2.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The relation $G$ is symmetric, so
$\Gm(G)=GG^{\mathsf T}=G^2$.  Every Boolean power of $G$ is symmetric;
induction therefore gives
\[
 \Gm(G^{2^s})=G^{2^s}(G^{2^s})^{\mathsf T}=G^{2^{s+1}},
\]
which proves \eqref{eq:iterate}.

For active vertices $i,j$, the entry $(G^r)_{ij}$ is one precisely when
there is a walk of length $r$ from $i$ to $j$.  Loops pad a shorter path to
any larger length, so this is equivalent to graph distance at most $r$.
Thus $G^{2^{t-1}}$ completes every active component exactly when
$2^{t-1}\geq D(G)$, and its stable value is the asserted partial
equivalence relation.  This proves the endpoint and \eqref{eq:clock},
including $D(G)=0,1$.

If $A=AA^{\mathsf T}$, then $A$ is symmetric, every nonempty row has its
diagonal entry, and $A=A^2$ is transitive.  Its active components are
therefore fully looped cliques.  Conversely, such a partial equivalence
relation $A$ is symmetric and idempotent, hence $AA^{\mathsf T}=A^2=A$.
Every orbit reaches one of these states, so there can be no other recurrent
state.  To count them, choose the $k$ active vertices and partition them
into clique components.  This gives the sum in \eqref{eq:bell}; adjoining a
distinguished element to the inactive vertices is the standard bijection
with partitions of an $(n+1)$-element set.  Every periodic point is fixed,
so $|\Fix(\Gm^r)|=B_{n+1}$ for every $r\geq1$, and the definition of the
Artin--Mazur zeta function gives \eqref{eq:zeta}.

Finally, $D(G)\leq n-1$, proving the upper bound in \eqref{eq:height}.  For
$n\geq2$, label the edges of the path $1-2-\cdots-n$ by
$1,\ldots,n-1$ and let row $i$ of $A$ contain the labels of the path edges
incident with $i$; leave column $n$ empty.  Then $AA^{\mathsf T}$ is the
fully looped path, of diameter $n-1$, and $A$ is not fixed.  Its depth is
the claimed bound.  At $n=1$, both Boolean matrices are fixed.
\end{proof}

\section{Every-target ordered-column fibres}

We now solve the one-step inverse problem without assuming that the target
lies in the image.  For a Boolean relation $H$ on $[n]$, define
\[
 \mathcal C(H)=\{C\subseteq[n]:H_{ij}=1\text{ for every }i,j\in C\}.
\]
Thus $\varnothing\in\mathcal C(H)$, and every nonempty member is a fully
looped clique of $H$.  When $H$ is symmetric and each edge has both endpoint
loops, let
\begin{equation}\label{eq:atoms}
 E^*(H)=\bigl\{\{i\}:H_{ii}=1\bigr\}
 \cup\bigl\{\{i,j\}:i<j,\ H_{ij}=1\bigr\}.
\end{equation}
For $S\subseteq E^*(H)$ put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cHS}
 c_H(S)=\#\{C\in\mathcal C(H): e\nsubseteq C
                         \text{ for every }e\in S\}.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[complete target fibre]\label{thm:fibre}
If $H$ is nonsymmetric, or if some entry $H_{ij}=1$ has $H_{ii}H_{jj}=0$,
then $\Gm^{-1}(H)=\varnothing$.  For every remaining target,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fibre}
 \boxed{\quad
 |\Gm^{-1}(H)|=
 \sum_{S\subseteq E^*(H)}(-1)^{|S|}\,c_H(S)^n.
 \quad}
\end{equation}
Moreover, $H$ lies in the image exactly when the atoms $E^*(H)$ can be
covered by at most $n$ members of $\mathcal C(H)\setminus\{\varnothing\}$,
where $C$ covers $e$ when $e\subseteq C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The necessary symmetry and loop conditions follow immediately from
\eqref{eq:intersection}.  Suppose they hold and write $C_r$ for the support
of column $r$ of a prospective source matrix $A$.  Boolean multiplication
gives the exact union identity
\begin{equation}\label{eq:square-union}
 AA^{\mathsf T}=\bigcup_{r=1}^{n} C_r\times C_r.
\end{equation}
No zero of $H$ is changed to one exactly when each
$C_r\in\mathcal C(H)$.  Every one of $H$ is obtained exactly when, for each
atom $e\in E^*(H)$, at least one chosen $C_r$ contains $e$.

For a fixed set $S$ of atoms, there are $c_H(S)^n$ ordered column sequences
that miss every atom of $S$.  Inclusion--exclusion over the events ``atom
$e$ is missed by all columns'' gives \eqref{eq:fibre}.  The columns are
labelled, so their order matters; equal column supports are allowed, and
the empty support is an allowed padding column.  Consequently such a
sequence exists precisely when at most $n$ nonempty allowed cliques cover
all atoms.
\end{proof}

Ignoring loops, the last statement specializes to the classical
intersection-number/edge-clique-cover criterion
\cite{ErdosGoodmanPosa1966}.  The singleton atoms in \eqref{eq:atoms} are
essential: an isolated looped vertex still needs a nonempty singleton
column support.  Compatibility alone is not sufficient.  For example, the
fully looped $K_{2,3}$ target at $n=5$ has six edges, every allowed
nontrivial clique is an edge, and hence its fibre is zero.

Some small fibres illustrate the ordered convention.  Let $I_3$ and $J_3$
be the identity and all-one relations, and let $P_3^\circ$ be the
three-vertex path with all three loops.  Formula \eqref{eq:fibre} gives
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
target $H$ & $0_3$ & $I_3$ & $P_3^\circ$ & $J_3$\\
\midrule
$|\Gamma_3^{-1}(H)|$ & $1$ & $6$ & $30$ & $175$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
For $0_n$, all columns must be empty, so the fibre is one.  For an isolated
single loop and $n-1$ inactive vertices, exactly a nonempty subset of the
$n$ labelled columns may equal that singleton, giving $2^n-1$.  These
examples also expose the zero target, isolated-loop, repeated-column, and
empty-column boundaries.

\section{Exact finite controls and scope}

A standalone standard-library verifier exhausts every source and every
target through $n=4$.  It compares literal fibres with
\eqref{eq:fibre}, checks the cover criterion, temporal identity, exact
diameter clock, fixed census, and sharp path witnesses; the latter are also
replayed through $n=64$.  Its frozen summaries are
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & states & image & fixed & max depth & max fibre\\
\midrule
1 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 0 & 1\\
2 & 16 & 5 & 5 & 1 & 7\\
3 & 512 & 18 & 15 & 2 & 175\\
4 & 65,536 & 113 & 52 & 3 & 17,887\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The same program explicitly exercises $n=1$, the zero matrix, the
$D(G)\leq1$ convention, isolated loops, unlooped incident-edge targets, and
empty columns.  Finite computation is evidence against transcription or
boundary errors, not a substitute for the all-parameter proofs above.

The claims deliberately stop at this exact package.  Boolean semiring
algebra, graph squaring and component closure, Bell enumeration,
intersection representations, edge clique covers, and inclusion--exclusion
are not claimed contributions.  In particular, Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}
does not solve the optimization or reconstruction variants of symmetric
Boolean factorization.  This document is an anonymous author-side Round-0
record with external status \texttt{HOLD\_EXTERNAL}; it makes no submission,
release, novelty, or priority assertion.

